 decreased by 1; reduced, while Prevotella and Actinomyces were enriched, along with suppression of nuc; decreased, and Clostridia increased, with significant upregulation of gluconeogenesis an; reduced caloric intake and dietary adjustments but also from shifts in gut microbial c; changes and host metabolic indicators such as body weight, body mass index (BMI), bloo; 24 kg/m; 2 kg/m; 4.75 kg/m2; 5.67 kg/m; 2%; 27.9 kg/m2; 28 kg/m2; decreases of 4; 1.60 kg","Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effects of modified fasting therapy on tongue coating and gut microbiome in overweight and obese adults: a controlled clinical trial Dongkai Zeng †, Tingying Zhang †, Yunling Zhu , Jiahao Feng , Ziheng Ye , Jin Zhao , Peng Huang , Li Zhang * and Taoli Liu * Traditional Chinese Medicine Department, The Seventh Affilia || Methods: To address this gap, we conducted a single-center, prospective, controlled study from 23 April to 5 July 2021, enrolling 48 participants with a body mass index (BMI) ≥ 24 kg/m2. || Results: In the fasting group, body weight decreased by 4.0 ± 1.6 kg (p < 0.01), BMI decreased by 1.51 ± 0.58 (p < 0.01), significantly, accompanied by marked improvements in blood glucose and lipid profiles (p < 0.05). || In the gut microbiota, Bacteroides decreased, and Clostridia increased, with significant upregulation of gluconeogenesis and branched-chain amino acid biosynthesis pathways ( p < 0.05). || Notably, specific taxa such as Haemophilus and Granulicatella were positively correlated with body weight and BMI (r > 0.4, p < 0.05). || KEYWORDS controlled clinical trial, gut microbiome, modified fasting therapy, overweight/obesity, tongue coating microbiota OPEN ACCESS EDITED BY Hanif Ullah, Sichuan University, China REVIEWED BY Sajid Ur Rahman, Southern University of Science and Technology, China Saqib Nawaz, Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences, China *CORRESPONDENCE Taoli Liu liutl3","Frontiers in Nutrition 01 frontiersin.org Effects of modified fasting 